End a session by updating the resume block before context clears.

## The working loop: one task, then stop for us

One task at a time; keep us in the loop after each. For each task: (1) state the task by id + the requirement/
acceptance; (2) **ground the calls** (engine against the `engine` lane, libraries against `project`) — show what
you found, never guess an API; (3) define/confirm the contract; (4) write the failing test(s) (TDD red) — **and a
LIVE test against the real engine on any engine-seam task** (fakes prove the mapping, only a live run proves the
integration); (5) implement the minimum to pass (green), this task only; (6) run the task's tests + verify, show
results + diff; (7) **stop and wait for our explicit approval** — do not mark done, do not start the next task;
(8) after approval only: mark done in `{{TASKS}}`, write an ADR if you made an architectural decision, update
docs, commit atomically. Never mark done without approval; never batch tasks.

## How to write the code

Think before coding; state assumptions; present options rather than silently picking one. Keep it simple — the
minimum that satisfies the task and the spec. Change surgically; match existing style. Parallelize multiple
LLM/model calls (async + semaphore) in evals/tests/scripts; never sequential.

## Long-running background work: stream X/N progress and monitor it (never launch-and-forget)

Any background task over ~30s (an ingest, an eval, a bulk LLM loop, a migration) MUST (1) emit periodic `X/N`
progress to its log (start line with total N, `[stage] i/N …` flushed, end summary) and (2) be actively monitored
and reported in `X/N` form (tail the log FILE; never pipe a live job through grep/tail). Never launch and sit
silently.

## Cross-session memory and decisions

- `{{TASKS}}` is the persistent ledger (id, requirement/acceptance, status, files, verify, one-line note; a
  "last approved / next up" summary at top). Update status only as the loop allows; `done` only after approval.
  Task ids are stable; execution order may be reordered without renumbering.
- `docs/adr/` holds short ADRs, one per architectural decision. Cite the ADR a task is shaped by; if a task would
  contradict one, stop and raise it. Engine-side decisions live in the engine repo's ADRs — cite by id.

## Version control

Start each task from a clean, committed tree. After approval, commit once atomically (code + `{{TASKS}}` status +
any ADR + docs), naming the task + its requirement/acceptance. Every main-line commit is green and reviewed. Never
commit secrets or generated data (`.env`, caches, `graphify-out/` stay gitignored). Commits are local; ask before
pushing to a shared remote. Keep changes small (~100 lines; ~300 max for one logical change); when a change grows,
land the small one and stack the next on top — don't widen. A change that both moves existing code and adds
behaviour is two changes: separate refactoring from feature work.

## Boundaries

- **Always:** a LIVE engine-seam test on every engine integration; ground engine/library calls before writing them,
  against the right lane; classify a new operation's orchestration mode before building it; cite every claim;
  state coverage alongside any result (what was measured, on how many, what was left out); run the task's tests
  before presenting; stream `X/N` + monitor long-running work; stop for approval after each task; commit each
  approved task from a clean tree.
- **Ask first:** changing `{{SPEC}}`; adding a dependency; changing a data model/schema; anything that needs an
  **engine** change (write the engine request); a run that spends money, runs over ~5 minutes, or deletes data
  (dropping a database cannot be undone).
- **Never:** modify the engine from this repo/session; work around an engine defect in product code; fork or copy
  engine code wholesale; reach past `rag_wright.api` / `rag_wright.pack_sdk` into engine internals; give product
  code an ArcadeDB client or point it at another product's or the engine's instance; name a model, vendor or vendor
  flag in code; put a model in the path of an unambiguous button click; treat the reference pack as your domain;
  silently omit a document from a sweep; mark a task done or start a new one without approval; run bare
  `python`/`pip`; commit secrets; let a claim out without a citation; suggest pausing/stopping as a recommendation
  (we decide when to pause — propose only substantive next steps, or state done and wait).

## Phases (each ends with our review gate)

- Phase 0, setup: repo scaffold, environment, this product's own ArcadeDB, grounding lanes, the engine seam
  (capability registration at startup), the Phase 0 guard tests (the playbook lists them), `{{SPEC}}` placeholders
  repaid.
- Phase 1, Plan: produce `{{PLAN}}` (read-only plan mode). We review.
- Phase 2, Tasks: produce `{{TASKS}}`, each mapped to its requirement/acceptance. We review.
- Phase 3, Contracts: define the product contracts. We review.
- Phase 4, Build: the working loop above, per task, each with our approval gate.
- Phase 5, Integrate + end to end: wire the surfaces + the engine, run the end-to-end scenarios, confirm
  acceptance. We review.
